===CONFUSING WORDS START===
Advanced (commonly mixed up)

Borrow vs Lend – borrow = take; lend = give. "Can I borrow your charger?" / "Can you lend me your charger?"
Affect vs Effect – affect = verb; effect = noun. "The outage affected users." / "The effect was minor."
Accept vs Except – accept = receive; except = excluding. "I accept the proposal, except section 3."
Advice vs Advise – advice = noun; advise = verb. "My advice: ship it." / "I'd advise waiting."
Practice vs Practise (UK) – noun vs verb. In US English, practice for both.
Its vs It's – its = possessive; it's = it is. "The service lost its config; it's down."
Then vs Than – then = time; than = comparison. "Faster than before, then we deploy."
Lose vs Loose – lose = misplace/fail; loose = not tight. "We'll lose data." / "Loose coupling."
Fewer vs Less – fewer = countable; less = uncountable. "Fewer bugs, less downtime."
Amount vs Number – amount = uncountable; number = countable. "Amount of traffic, number of requests."
Farther vs Further – farther = physical distance; further = abstract. "Further discussion needed."
Complement vs Compliment – complement = completes; compliment = praise. "The tools complement each other." / "He complimented the design."
Principal vs Principle – principal = main/person; principle = rule. "Principal engineer" / "design principle."
Stationary vs Stationery – stationary = not moving; stationery = paper supplies.
Ensure vs Insure vs Assure – ensure = make certain; insure = insurance; assure = reassure someone. "Ensure tests pass; I assure you it's safe."
Lay vs Lie – lay takes an object ("lay the cable"); lie doesn't ("the file lies in /tmp").
Bring vs Take – bring = toward speaker; take = away. "Bring the laptop here; take it to the meeting."
Say vs Tell – tell needs a person. "Tell me the status." / "Say the status." (no "tell the status")
Remember vs Remind – remind = cause someone to remember. "Remind me to send the PR."
Learn vs Teach – learn = receive knowledge; teach = give it. Never "he learned me Go."
Job vs Work – job = countable position; work = uncountable activity. "A new job, lots of work."
Trip vs Journey vs Travel – trip = the whole visit; journey = the moving part; travel = general/uncountable.
Historic vs Historical – historic = important; historical = from the past. "A historic release; historical data."
Economic vs Economical – economic = about the economy; economical = cost-saving. "An economical approach."
Sensitive vs Sensible – sensitive = easily affected/private; sensible = reasonable. "Sensitive data; a sensible plan."

Very advanced (even natives slip)

Imply vs Infer – speaker implies; listener infers. "You implied it's late; I inferred a delay."
Comprise vs Compose – the whole comprises the parts; the parts compose the whole. "The system comprises five services." (never "comprised of")
Continual vs Continuous – continual = repeated with breaks; continuous = nonstop. "Continual interruptions; continuous deployment."
Disinterested vs Uninterested – disinterested = impartial; uninterested = bored. "A disinterested reviewer."
Nauseous vs Nauseated – strictly, nauseous = causing nausea; nauseated = feeling it.
Elicit vs Illicit – elicit = draw out; illicit = illegal. "Elicit feedback."
Allude vs Elude – allude = hint at; elude = escape. "He alluded to layoffs; the bug eludes us."
Discreet vs Discrete – discreet = careful/private; discrete = separate. "Discrete modules; be discreet about the deal."
Precede vs Proceed – precede = come before; proceed = continue. "Validation precedes deployment; then proceed."
Adverse vs Averse – adverse = harmful; averse = opposed. "Adverse effects; risk-averse."
Eminent vs Imminent – eminent = famous; imminent = about to happen. "The release is imminent."
Flaunt vs Flout – flaunt = show off; flout = ignore rules. "He flouted the code style."
Enervate vs Energize – enervate = drain energy (opposite of what it sounds).
Exhaustive vs Exhausting – exhaustive = complete; exhausting = tiring. "An exhaustive test suite."
Ambiguous vs Ambivalent – ambiguous = unclear meaning; ambivalent = mixed feelings. "The spec is ambiguous; I'm ambivalent about it."
Alternate vs Alternative – alternate = every other; alternative = another option. "Alternate weeks; an alternative design."
Assure vs Ensure – (see 15) assure a person, ensure an outcome.
Compliant vs Compliment – compliant = follows rules. "GDPR-compliant."
Dependent vs Dependant – dependent = adjective; dependant (UK) = noun (person).
Prescribe vs Proscribe – prescribe = recommend; proscribe = forbid. Opposites!
Tortuous vs Torturous – tortuous = winding/complex; torturous = painful. "A tortuous codebase."
Ingenious vs Ingenuous – ingenious = clever; ingenuous = naive.
Regretfully vs Regrettably – regretfully = with regret (the person); regrettably = unfortunately (the situation).
Peruse – actually means read carefully, not skim.
Literally – means actually, not "very." "The server literally caught fire" only if it did.


Advanced

Who vs Whom – who = subject; whom = object. "Who approved it? To whom was it sent?"
Which vs That – that = essential info; which = extra info (with comma). "The test that failed" / "The test, which was new, failed."
Lose vs Loss – lose = verb; loss = noun. "We may lose data; data loss is the risk."
Choose vs Chose – present vs past. "I chose Go; I'd choose it again."
Quiet vs Quite – quiet = silent; quite = fairly. "Quite a quiet release."
Breath vs Breathe – noun vs verb.
Cloth vs Clothes – cloth = material; clothes = garments.
Desert vs Dessert – desert = sand; dessert = sweet.
Personal vs Personnel – personal = private; personnel = staff.
Council vs Counsel – council = a group; counsel = advice / to advise.
Device vs Devise – noun vs verb. "Devise a plan for the device."
Envelop vs Envelope – verb (wrap) vs noun.
Bear vs Bare – bear = carry/tolerate; bare = uncovered. "Bear with me" (not "bare").
Passed vs Past – passed = verb; past = time/beyond. "Tests passed; past results."
Weather vs Whether – climate vs "if."
Whose vs Who's – possessive vs "who is."
Your vs You're – possessive vs "you are."
Their vs There vs They're – possessive / place / "they are."
Later vs Latter – later = time; latter = second of two. "Of REST and gRPC, I prefer the latter."
Beside vs Besides – beside = next to; besides = in addition.
Everyday vs Every day – everyday = adjective (ordinary); every day = each day. "Everyday tasks happen every day."
Anytime vs Any time – anytime = adverb; "at any time" needs two words.
Setup vs Set up – noun vs verb. "Set up the setup."
Login vs Log in – noun vs verb. Same pattern: backup / back up, rollout / roll out.
Sometime vs Some time vs Sometimes – a point in time / a duration / occasionally.

Very advanced

Adapt vs Adopt – adapt = adjust; adopt = take on. "We adopted Go and adapted our tooling."
Access vs Excess – entry vs too much.
Appraise vs Apprise – appraise = evaluate; apprise = inform. "Apprise the team of the change."
Censor vs Sensor vs Censure – block content / a detector / formal criticism.
Cite vs Site vs Sight – quote / location / vision.
Climactic vs Climatic – climax vs climate.
Collaborate vs Corroborate – work together vs confirm.
Contemptuous vs Contemptible – showing contempt vs deserving it.
Credible vs Credulous – believable vs too willing to believe.
Defuse vs Diffuse – defuse = calm/remove danger; diffuse = spread out. "Defuse the tension."
Deprecate vs Depreciate – deprecate = mark as obsolete (tech); depreciate = lose value (finance).
Emigrate vs Immigrate – leave a country vs enter one.
Explicit vs Implicit – stated clearly vs implied.
Formally vs Formerly – officially vs previously.
Hoard vs Horde – stockpile vs crowd.
Incredible vs Incredulous – unbelievable vs disbelieving.
Loath vs Loathe – loath = reluctant; loathe = hate. "I'm loath to refactor now."
Moral vs Morale – ethics vs team spirit. "Team morale is low."
Perspective vs Prospective – viewpoint vs potential/future. "A prospective client."
Persecute vs Prosecute – oppress vs take to court.
Pore vs Pour – pore over = study closely. "Pore over the logs" (not "pour").
Reticent vs Reluctant – reticent = not talkative; reluctant = unwilling.
Simple vs Simplistic – simplistic is negative: oversimplified. "That's a simplistic view."
Uninterested vs Disinterested – (from earlier list; worth repeating — it's the most misused)
Verbal vs Oral – verbal = in words (spoken or written); oral = spoken only.

===CONFUSING WORDS ENDS===

===CONVESATIONS START===
Advanced

1. Sprint planning — negotiating scope

PM: Can we fit the export feature into this sprint too?
You: I'd love to say yes, but let me be realistic. We're already at capacity with the runtime work. If export is a must-have, something has to give — either we drop the metrics dashboard or we push the runtime milestone by a week.
PM: The dashboard can wait.
You: Okay, let's swap them. I'll update the board and flag the change in the channel so no one is caught off guard.

2. Code review disagreement

Colleague: I don't think we need tests for this helper, it's trivial.
You: I hear you, and normally I'd agree. My concern is that this helper sits on the hot path — if it regresses, we won't notice until prod. A couple of table-driven tests would take ten minutes and give us a safety net.
Colleague: Fair. I'll add them.
You: Appreciate it. Everything else looks clean — nice work on the error handling.

3. Being asked something you don't know in front of seniors

Architect: What's the p99 latency after your change?
You: I don't have the exact figure in front of me and I'd rather not guess. My recollection is roughly a 30% improvement, but let me pull the dashboard and confirm right after this call.
Architect: Fine. Send it in the thread.
You: Will do.

4. Handing over work before leave

You: I'm out next week, so I want to make sure nothing falls through the cracks. Vaibhav, could you be the point of contact for the scheduler? I've documented the open items in the wiki and left comments on the two pending PRs.
Vaibhav: Sure. Anything urgent?
You: Only the vendor call on Wednesday — I've briefed Srini on it. If anything else comes up, use your judgment; I trust your call.

Very advanced

5. Pushing back on an unrealistic deadline from leadership

Director: We need this shipped by end of month. The customer is expecting it.
You: I understand the commitment, and I want to help you meet it. I also owe you an honest assessment: with the current scope, end of month means shipping without load testing, and that's a material risk for a customer of this size.
Director: What are my options?
You: Three. One, we ship a reduced scope on time — core flow only. Two, we keep full scope and land it two weeks later with confidence. Three, we add one more engineer and I'll re-estimate by tomorrow. My recommendation is option one; it protects the relationship and the timeline.
Director: Let's go with one. Put it in writing.
You: I'll send a summary today with what's in, what's deferred, and the risks we're accepting.

6. Disagreeing with your manager — and committing anyway

Manager: We're going with the third-party orchestration library instead of building our own.
You: Can I share a concern before we lock it in? My worry is vendor lock-in — their API has changed twice this year, and we'd be building the core of the product on it.
Manager: Noted, but we don't have the bandwidth to build it ourselves.
You: That's a fair constraint. I'll disagree and commit. Two things I'd ask: we wrap the library behind our own interface so we can swap it later, and we revisit the decision in Q1 with real data.
Manager: Both reasonable.
You: Then I'm fully on board. I'll lead the integration.

7. Receiving critical feedback

Manager: In the last review, a few people felt your updates were hard to follow — too much detail, not enough context.
You: Thank you for telling me directly; I'd rather hear it than not. That resonates, actually — I tend to lead with the technical weeds. Could you give me one concrete example so I can calibrate?
Manager: The migration update last Thursday.
You: Got it. Going forward I'll open with the headline — status, risk, ask — and keep details for those who want them. Would you be open to giving me quick feedback after the next two updates?
Manager: Happy to.

8. Escalating a cross-team blocker without blaming anyone

You: I want to raise something before it becomes a bigger problem. We've been waiting eight days on the auth team for the token API, and our milestone is now at risk.
Manager: Have you spoken to them?
You: Twice, and to be fair, they're stretched thin with their own release. I'm not looking to point fingers — I'm looking for a path forward. Options as I see them: we get a firm date from their lead, we build a temporary stub and proceed, or you and their manager agree on priority. I'd suggest the stub plus a date, so we're not blocked either way.
Manager: I'll talk to their manager today.
You: Thanks. I'll start the stub in parallel and keep both of you posted.


1. Rejecting a feature request from a big customer
Customer: We need custom retry logic per workflow by next release.
You: I appreciate you being specific about the need. I want to be transparent: per-workflow retries would touch the core scheduler, and we can't do that safely in one release. What I can offer is a global retry policy now, and we'll scope the per-workflow version for Q1. Would that unblock you in the meantime?

2. Taking ownership of a production incident
Manager: What happened last night?
You: A change I merged introduced a nil dereference under a rare race condition. I take full responsibility. We rolled back within twelve minutes, no data was lost, and I've already opened a postmortem. The systemic gap is that our integration tests don't cover concurrent cancellation — I'll fix that this week so it can't recur.

3. Asking for a raise
You: I'd like to discuss my compensation. Over the past year I've taken ownership of the runtime engine, mentored two engineers, and reduced p99 latency by forty percent. Based on market data and the scope I'm carrying, I believe my pay is behind where it should be. What would it take to close that gap?
Manager: I'll need to look at the bands.
You: Understood. Could we agree on a timeline — say, a follow-up in two weeks?

4. Declining extra work without sounding lazy
Lead: Can you also pick up the migration tickets?
You: I want to be useful here, so let me be honest about capacity. I'm at full load with the runtime milestone, and taking those on would put both at risk. If the migration is the higher priority, I'm happy to swap — but I'd rather you make that call than have me quietly under-deliver on both.

5. Interrupting a senior person who is wrong
VP: The latency issue is clearly the database.
You: Sorry to jump in — I want to make sure we don't chase the wrong thing. The profiling from Tuesday actually points to serialization on our side; the DB was under five milliseconds throughout. I can share the flame graph after this if that helps.

6. Giving hard feedback to a peer
You: Do you have a few minutes? I want to share something, and I'm raising it because I respect your work. In the last two reviews, the comments came across as dismissive — "this is obviously wrong" rather than "here's what I'd change." I don't think that's your intent, but it's landing that way with the juniors.
Peer: I didn't realize.
You: I figured. Happy to look over comments together if that's useful.

7. Presenting a proposal to leadership
You: I'll keep this to three points. The problem: our agent runtime can't scale beyond ten concurrent graphs. The proposal: move to a work-stealing scheduler, which our prototype shows handles two hundred. The ask: two engineers for six weeks. The main risk is migration complexity, and we'd mitigate that with a feature flag and a phased rollout. Happy to take questions.

8. Handling a hostile question in a demo
Skeptic: This looks like a toy. How does it handle real failure?
You: That's the right question to ask. Let me show you rather than tell you — I'll kill a worker mid-execution now. As you can see, the graph resumes from the last checkpoint. That said, we haven't tested at ten thousand nodes yet, and I'd rather be upfront about that than overclaim.

9. Negotiating with a vendor
Vendor: The enterprise tier is forty thousand a year.
You: That's above what we've budgeted. To be candid, we're evaluating two alternatives. What I can commit to is a three-year term and a public case study — is there room to move on price for that, or on including the premium support tier?

10. Managing up when your manager is disorganized
You: I've noticed priorities have shifted a few times this month, and I want to make sure I'm working on what matters most to you. Would it help if I sent you a short weekly note — top three items, what's at risk, what I need from you? That way we stay aligned without extra meetings.

11. Onboarding a new team member
You: Welcome aboard. My goal for your first two weeks isn't output — it's context. I've set up three things: a walkthrough of the architecture on Monday, a small starter ticket so you can see the full PR flow, and a standing thirty-minute slot with me every other day. Ask anything, however basic; the only bad question is the one that costs you a day.

12. Admitting you were wrong in a debate
You: I've been thinking about our discussion, and I want to close the loop. You were right about the caching approach — I was optimizing for a case that doesn't happen in practice. I've updated the design doc to reflect your version. Thanks for pushing on it.

13. Escalating a security concern
You: I need to flag something that can't wait. The staging environment is using production credentials in a config that's committed to the repo. I've rotated the keys and removed the file, but we should assume exposure. I'd recommend we notify security today and audit the last thirty days of access. I'm happy to write it up.

14. Convincing a reluctant team to adopt a change
You: I know there's fatigue around process changes, so let me be clear about what this isn't: it isn't more meetings. It's one linting rule that catches the class of bug that bit us three times last quarter. Let's trial it for two weeks; if it's more noise than signal, we drop it, no argument from me.

15. Handling credit being taken by someone else
Manager: Great work on the scheduler, Rijo.
You: Rijo did drive the rollout, and it went smoothly. For context, the scheduler design and implementation were mine — I mention it only because it's relevant to what I'll be picking up next. Happy to walk through the design if useful.

16. Delivering a layoff or bad news to a junior
You: I want to talk to you directly rather than have you hear it elsewhere. Your contract won't be renewed at the end of the quarter. This is a budget decision, not a performance one — your work has been solid, and I'll say so in any reference. What I can do is give you time during work hours for interviews and introduce you to two teams I know are hiring.

17. Running a blameless postmortem
You: Ground rule first: we're here to fix the system, not assign fault. Anyone in the same position with the same information would likely have made the same call. So, walking the timeline: alert fired at 02:14, the on-call engineer saw a misleading dashboard and restarted the wrong service. The real question is why the dashboard was misleading. Let's start there.

18. Responding to an unfair performance review
You: Thank you for the review. I want to engage with it seriously, and there are two points I'd like to discuss. The rating on "delivery" cites the delayed migration — that delay was caused by the auth dependency, which I escalated twice in writing. I'd ask that the context be reflected. On "communication," I accept the feedback fully and have already changed my update format. Can we revisit the delivery rating with that context?

19. Persuading a stakeholder to cut scope
Stakeholder: Everything in the spec is essential.
You: If everything is essential, then effectively nothing is prioritized, and that's what worries me. Let me propose a different lens: which three items would the customer notice missing on day one? Those we ship first. Everything else isn't cut — it's sequenced.

20. Resigning gracefully
You: I wanted to tell you in person before anything formal. I've accepted an offer elsewhere and my last day will be in four weeks. This isn't about anything going wrong here — it's a role I couldn't turn down. I'll make sure the runtime handover is thorough; I've already drafted a transition plan and I'd like your input on who takes it over.
===CONVESATIONS END===